  "whyItMatters": "A rigid end offset says where the flexible length of a member starts: the part inside the joint (half the depth of the column or beam it frames into) does not bend. It shortens the flexible span and moves the point where the member end forces act on the joint, which adds the moment V x e to the joint. On the wrong axis the offset no longer shortens the member along its axis: it shifts the flexible end sideways or vertically, which introduces an eccentricity (axial force x offset = moment, shear x offset = torsion) and changes the member's direction slightly.",
  "precondition": "a member with a nonzero rigid end offset",
